fact WorkItem(id: "WI-960", description: some(value: "make_type_var re-resolves a 41-char qualified name per unbound type param, per constructor node.\n\nreconstruct_sort_params (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs) mints a fresh `?_` wildcard for every param the field unification left UNBOUND (WI-384 - keep the sort's full param arity, never drop): `kb.intern(\"?_\")` then `kb.make_type_var(name)`. Both constructor-typing paths reach it - the occurrence typer (check_constructor_iter) and the value typer (constructor_value_type) - so it is per constructor node. That is the same path WI-657(7) turned into an O(1) index read precisely because per-node work there is measurable.\n\nVERIFIED, make_type_var (kb/mod.rs:6200):\n  resolve_symbol(\"anthill.prelude.TypeExtractor.TypeVar\")   <- hash probe over a 41-char qualified name\n  intern(\"name\") + alloc(Term::Ref(name)) + make_entity_term  <- and a hash-cons\n\nIts result is fully determined by `name`, and every call from this site passes the same `\"?_\"`, so the whole call is one constant TermId there. Nothing about it is per-node.\n\nPRE-EXISTING, not introduced by WI-955 - both of the pre-WI-955 branches did it identically and the fix only moved it into the shared tail. Surfaced by WI-955's /simplify efficiency pass, which measured it as materially more per-call work than the by_parent lookup that same change optimized.\n\nSCOPE: cache the TypeExtractor.TypeVar symbol on the KB the way op_records / sort_alias_index / sort_info_index already cache their lookups, or memoize make_type_var by name. Check the OTHER make_type_var callers first - if they mostly pass a varying name, the symbol cache is the win and the by-name memo is not.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven - assert the returned TermId is unchanged (it is hash-consed, so a wrong cache shows up as a different term), plus a stated before/after measurement of a stdlib type-check. Say at the test site which assertion fails when the cache is backed out. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-955 /simplify efficiency review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Rejected(reason: "Premise refuted by measurement, before implementing. make_type_var is called 6 times in a full stdlib + host-bindings load and type-check (3x \"?_\", 2x \"?param\", 1x \"?logical_var\"), and a 200x driver built specifically to force reconstruct_sort_params' unbound-param branch added ZERO further calls. Caching the TypeExtractor.TypeVar symbol would save ~6 hash probes inside a ~440ms load - one part in a million - for a KnowledgeBase field that must be kept correct. The ticket is right about where the code sits (per constructor node) and wrong about how often it runs. The by-name memo it also offered was never safe: TermStore is refcounted with a free_list and alloc increments even on a hash-cons HIT, so a cached TermId can dangle and returning one without an incref over-decrements every caller. See the feedback entry for the full numbers and for the representation question this should have asked instead (make_type_var returns a TermId, so transient inference wildcards are hash-consed into the PERSISTENT store; 8 of 11 production callers immediately wrap it in Value::term).", at: "2026-08-03T12:27:13Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-960", author: "user", content: "MEASURED BEFORE IMPLEMENTING - THIS TICKET'S PREMISE IS REFUTED. Status left Open pending a decision; nothing was changed in the tree.\n\nInstrumented make_type_var with a call counter, full stdlib + anthill-stl host-bindings load and type-check:\n  bare stdlib load                          6 calls   (3x \"?_\", 2x \"?param\", 1x \"?logical_var\")\n  same load + a 200x driver on the UNBOUND-param path   6 calls  (UNCHANGED)\n  load wall time                            ~440,000 us\n\nThe driver was built specifically to force reconstruct_sort_params' WI-384 unbound branch - 'sort Pair2 { sort A = ?; sort B = ?; entity onlyA(fst: A) }' applied 200 times, so B is left unbound by the field unification - and it added ZERO calls. The three \"?_\" calls come from fresh_type_var, not from the reconstruction tail this ticket names.\n\nSo the ticket is RIGHT about where the code sits (the tail is per constructor node) and WRONG about how often it runs. Caching the TypeExtractor.TypeVar symbol would save ~6 hash probes inside a 440ms load - about one part in a million - in exchange for a KnowledgeBase field that has to be kept correct. Not worth doing as specified. This is the same trap WI-881 recorded: a code path being on a hot line is not evidence that it executes.\n\nTWO THINGS FOUND WHILE MEASURING, both worth more than the optimization:\n\n1. THE by-name MEMO ALTERNATIVE IS UNSAFE, and this ticket should not have offered it. TermStore is refcounted with a free_list, and alloc() increments the refcount even on a hash-cons HIT (term.rs, the doc on alloc says so explicitly). So a memoized TermId can dangle after a free, and returning a cached id WITHOUT an incref would over-decrement every caller that later drops it. Only the symbol-cache half was ever safe - and that half is the one the measurement says is pointless.\n\n2. THE PREMISE THIS TICKET SHOULD HAVE QUESTIONED IS THE RETURN TYPE. make_type_var returns a TermId, so every transient inference wildcard is hash-consed AND refcounted into the PERSISTENT term store. CLAUDE.md's representation note reserves hash-consing for persistent, heavily-shared structure and says transient terms (query patterns, occurrence twins) are deliberately NOT interned - they ride as Value::Node/Entity and match structurally. A \"?_\" standing for an under-determined type is transient by definition. MEASURED at the call sites: 8 of 11 production callers immediately write Value::term(kb.make_type_var(fresh)) - the TermId is incidental there. Only three want a term: TypeChild::Ground (typing.rs:2059) and the two in reconstruct_sort_params, which feed make_parameterized_type building a hash-consed type spine. That is a REPRESENTATION question, not a performance one, and it is what made the memo unsafe in the first place.\n\nSOURCE of the correction: user asked 'why we use TermId in WI-960?' - the same question that dissolved this ticket's framing.", at: "2026-08-03T12:23:34Z")
